Hi Alexandre, hi all,

I've reworked the per-VM D-Bus foundation from Alexandre's v2 and moved
QSM's WebRTC console onto it. The original D-Bus patch retains Alexandre's
authorship and sign-off. qemu-server owns the bus and its lifetime; the
media service remains a separate package and attaches as a consumer.

The proposed opt-in is a VM-wide dbus-display: 1. GPU type and rendernode
remain in vga, and audio0 can select driver=dbus. CPU displays can keep
VNC alongside D-Bus; virtio-gl cannot offer VNC/SPICE alongside it.

I've separated the review into six common qemu-server patches, two
WebRTC API patches above them, and three pve-manager patches. WebRTC offers,
minimal VM status and codec settings use PVE's existing authenticated
API. Console access/read settings require VM.Console; policy writes
require VM.Config.Options. A pve-guest-common patch extracts the shared
local service transport, and a pve-container patch adds the equivalent
LXC API. Native VM and LXC signalling no longer needs port 8007.

On the installed packages I checked Firefox video and saving the existing
media policy for an LXC container, and VM video/Reconnect, with the legacy
HTTPS service stopped. Unauthenticated reads and writes without a CSRF
token are rejected. Graphical container sessions and encoding remain in
the console provider; the stock LXC terminal console is preserved.

The packages build with the upstream tests. In an isolated PVE 9 lab I
checked CPU and GL display, embedded and popup consoles together, sound
and microphone, clipboard in both directions and the API permissions.
A second independent D-Bus capture consumer also ran while the browser
continued decoding. The migration tool converts stopped QSM-managed VMs,
saves a private backup and preserves unrelated arguments/guest channels.

I've also adapted Markus Frank's Venus v4 configuration patch separately
and tested its command generation. It is optional; I have not qualified
Vulkan inside a guest. The NVIDIA EGL readback fix is kept separately too:
its adapter-to-file check is not a full Kyber/RDP browser qualification.

I'd appreciate feedback on the VM-wide option, bus ownership/lifecycle,
audio defaults, and the API/UI split before treating these as final.
The accompanying series contain the source bases and validation details.

Thanks,
Dmitry
